[{:db/id "q-343359/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "9361c8ef-7c6f-5641-92e3-529c4bd73ee7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-343359/field-1/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "ebea3bcd-7b13-570f-aed5-355dbce42f4e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-343359/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "c2c45fd4-c84b-59ea-8441-615248d9de44" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-343359/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer-field/choices ["q-343359/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-343359/field-1/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-343359" :question/id #uuid "2f4d1fb3-7b3b-5580-be3a-f087c43a017b" :question/math-academy-id "q-343359" :question/problem "$$\nF(x)={\\begin{aligned}-1, & x\\in [-\\pi ,0] \\\\ 3, & x\\in (0,\\pi )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. Find the value to which the Fourier series of the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ converges when $x=\\pi .$\n\nThe Fourier series converges to {{field-1}} when $x=\\pi .$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a $2L$-periodic piecewise smooth function with the corresponding Fourier series\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x).\n$$\n\nThen, the series converges for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x,$ then the series converges to the value of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=f(x)\n$$\n\n- If $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x,$ then the series converges to the midpoint between the values of left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{f}^{-}(x)+{f}^{+}(x)}{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{-}(x)$ and ${f}^{+}(x)$ are the left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point, respectively.\n\nNotice that the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of our function $F(x)$ is not continuous at $x=\\pi .$ The one-sided limits at this point are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-}(\\pi ) & ={lim}_{x\\to {\\pi }^{-}}f(x)=3 \\\\ {f}^{+}(\\pi ) & ={lim}_{x\\to {\\pi }^{+}}f(x)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ converges to \n\n$$\n\\frac{{f}^{-}(\\pi )+{f}^{+}(\\pi )}{2}=\\frac{3+(-1)}{2}=1.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-343359/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f9dcbb0-d943-5340-916e-d789b6ab03bc"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-343359"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-343390/field-1/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer/id #uuid "712615ce-7e0f-531a-af16-6d392cd5b2dd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-343390/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "e2dea436-1b87-5e8a-a4de-a642a6a48da7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-343390/field-1/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer-field/choices ["q-343390/field-1/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-343390" :question/id #uuid "43f8bb05-3ed3-5308-b000-5ac521338aa0" :question/math-academy-id "q-343390" :question/problem "$$\nF(x)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-4, & x\\in [-5,0] \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x, & x\\in (0,5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. Find the value to which the Fourier series of the $10$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is converging when $x=-3.$\n\nThe Fourier series converges to {{field-1}} when $x=-3.$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a $2L$-periodic piecewise smooth function with the corresponding Fourier series\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x).\n$$\n\nThen, the series converges for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x,$ then the series converges to the value of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=f(x)\n$$\n\n- If $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x,$ then the series converges to the midpoint between the values of left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{f}^{-}(x)+{f}^{+}(x)}{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{-}(x)$ and ${f}^{+}(x)$ are the left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point, respectively.\n\nNotice that the $10$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of our function $F(x)$ is continuous at $x=-3.$ \n\nTherefore, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ converges to \n\n$$\nf(-3)=(-3)^{2}-4=5.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-343390/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f9dcbb0-d943-5340-916e-d789b6ab03bc"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-343390"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-343385/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "8e7d5eb9-32f5-5714-92a1-4bc777fb024c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-343385/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "7b55c7f6-1f78-5a59-af9a-f873a89e6090" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-343385/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "afef0fa5-c466-53d4-949e-d53abd5ac927" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-343385/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer-field/choices ["q-343385/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-343385/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-343385" :question/id #uuid "ac3ad99a-9bdc-536e-b6f9-3e3c354ecd86" :question/math-academy-id "q-343385" :question/problem "$$\nF(x)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}, & x\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{3},0] \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x, & x\\in (0,\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. Find the value to which the Fourier series of the $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is converging when $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\nThe Fourier series converges to {{field-1}} when $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a $2L$-periodic piecewise smooth function with the corresponding Fourier series\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x).\n$$\n\nThen, the series converges for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x,$ then the series converges to the value of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=f(x)\n$$\n\n- If $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x,$ then the series converges to the midpoint between the values of left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{f}^{-}(x)+{f}^{+}(x)}{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{-}(x)$ and ${f}^{-}(x)$ are the left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point, respectively.\n\nNotice that the $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of our function $F(x)$ is continuous at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ \n\nTherefore, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ converges to \n\n$$\nf\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=2sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=1.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-343385/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f9dcbb0-d943-5340-916e-d789b6ab03bc"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-343385"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-343372/field-1/7f2aea1619f9c4cbe5384e0826442d82ebdb1ac614452244ced54b63c0c77efe" :answer/id #uuid "734e38b7-8ef7-54e2-aa1a-7b5d0d359619" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-343372/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "d5d74f00-5fd9-57c4-b9db-3e444160e5b5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-343372/field-1/7f2aea1619f9c4cbe5384e0826442d82ebdb1ac614452244ced54b63c0c77efe" :answer-field/choices ["q-343372/field-1/7f2aea1619f9c4cbe5384e0826442d82ebdb1ac614452244ced54b63c0c77efe"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-343372" :question/id #uuid "aba9a3ef-4052-574a-9c32-620903775160" :question/math-academy-id "q-343372" :question/problem "$$\nF(x)={\\begin{aligned}-2x+1, & x\\in [-4,0] \\\\ {x}^{2}+2, & x\\in (0,4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. Find the value to which the Fourier series of the $8$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is converging when $x=-3.$\n\nThe Fourier series converges to {{field-1}} when $x=-3.$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a $2L$-periodic piecewise smooth function with the corresponding Fourier series\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x).\n$$\n\nThen, the series converges for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x,$ then the series converges to the value of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=f(x)\n$$\n\n- If $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x,$ then the series converges to the midpoint between the values of left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{f}^{-}(x)+{f}^{+}(x)}{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{-}(x)$ and ${f}^{+}(x)$ are the left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point, respectively.\n\nNotice that the $8$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of our function $F(x)$ is continuous at $x=-3.$ \n\nTherefore, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ converges to \n\n$$\nf(-3)=-2(-3)+1=7.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-343372/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f9dcbb0-d943-5340-916e-d789b6ab03bc"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-343372"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-343370/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "3827078d-78ef-50d4-95c4-49bcda08d0a3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-343370/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "f5cfbe10-fc91-5457-9548-860416157604" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-343370/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer-field/choices ["q-343370/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-343370" :question/id #uuid "51238f35-c17c-5a3e-a012-9f14f97356f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-343370" :question/problem "$$\nF(x)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}, & x\\in [-\\pi ,0] \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+1, & x\\in (0,\\pi )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. Find the value to which the Fourier series of the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is converging when $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nThe Fourier series converges to {{field-1}} when $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a $2L$-periodic piecewise smooth function with the corresponding Fourier series\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x).\n$$\n\nThen, the series converges for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x,$ then the series converges to the value of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=f(x)\n$$\n\n- If $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x,$ then the series converges to the midpoint between the values of left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{f}^{-}(x)+{f}^{+}(x)}{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{-}(x)$ and ${f}^{+}(x)$ are the left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point, respectively.\n\nNotice that the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of our function $F(x)$ is continuous at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nTherefore, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ converges to \n\n$$\nf\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+1=2.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-343370/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f9dcbb0-d943-5340-916e-d789b6ab03bc"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-343370"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-343429/field-1/f059da041eb9c10af5c202e3f23518c1e88e6519cb516e4f5c2cc5a22f2b8121" :answer/id #uuid "36ca6605-1b90-574b-a7d7-4513918444d4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-1)^{n}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-343429/field-1/6176e2b047be2fa55f356f4eb746238d6c1d1572019aff3fe49b618179d289cd" :answer/id #uuid "bc424c58-aa60-5fed-8eb2-8cff4f97d2e9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-1)^{n+1}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-343429/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "209a16d7-3950-553a-97f7-aa8a95bab55e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-343429/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "a67aa225-130d-5a90-9ec1-55a7b87e041f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-343429/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "0f49ed5b-0fd0-5aaa-8919-c8c48c59